      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Using The slice() Method"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "If you omit the second parameter in slice(), it will extract the substring from the start index to the end of the string."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "1let movie = \"Snowpiercer\";\n2\n3let slicedText = movie.slice(5);\n4\n5console.log(slicedText);    // Output: iercer"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, movie.slice(5) extracts the substring starting from index 5 to the end of the string, resulting in \"iercer\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Using Negative Value"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "You can also use negative values to count from the end of the string."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "content": "1let movie = \"Snowpiercer\";\n2\n3let slicedText = movie.slice(-3);\n4\n5console.log(slicedText);    // Output: cer"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Here, movie.slice(-3) starts extracting from 3 characters from the end, giving you \"cer\"."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
